     "id": "notes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "The model behind [Search and Matching Equilibrium Explorer](note:search-and-matching-equilibrium-explorer). Place the view, not this note."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Model.** The [Diamond-Mortensen-Pissarides Model](note:diamond-mortensen-pissarides-model) with matching elasticity $\\alpha = 1/2$. Each curve is written as tightness: vacancy supply $\\theta = \\left(A(y - w)/(\\kappa\\lambda)\\right)^2$ and wage setting $\\theta = (w - \\beta y - (1-\\beta)b)/(\\beta\\kappa)$ as functions of the wage, and the [Beveridge Curve](note:beveridge-curve) $\\theta = \\left(\\lambda(1-u)/(Au)\\right)^2$ as a function of the unemployment rate. Equating VS and WS gives a quadratic in $\\sqrt{\\theta}$, solved in closed form.\n- **Baseline.** `vs_base`, `ws_base`, `bc_base` and `theta0`, `w0`, `u0` hold the practice numbers, so a view can show a curve before and after a shift.\n- **Tests.** The practice numbers and six one-parameter changes, solved in Python by the quadratic and checked with a numerical root-finder; curve values checked at fixed $w$ and $u$."
      }
     ],
